.TH TERMINAL-SYSTEM 1 "2026-09-06" "terminal-system 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
terminal-system \- AI-assisted two-pane terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B terminal-system
[\fIclaude\fR|\fIcodex\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opens a tmux session with two panes. The left pane is a normal shell
where commands run. The right pane accepts instructions in natural
language; the chosen AI engine (Claude Code or Codex, called
non-interactively with no tool access of its own) translates each
instruction into one or more shell commands, which are typed into the
left pane in real time.
.SH RIGHT-PANE COMMANDS
.TP
.B :cmd <command>
Type a shell command manually, without calling the AI engine.
.TP
.B :manual
Prompt for a manual shell command.
.TP
.B :auto on|off
Toggle automatic execution of generated commands. Default: off.
.TP
.B :engine claude|codex
Switch the AI engine for the rest of the session.
.TP
.B :quit
End the session.
.SH SAFETY
Every generated or manual command waits for manual confirmation by
default. Press Enter to execute it, "e" to edit it, or "n" to cancel it.
In auto-mode, a fixed set of dangerous patterns (destructive rm, mkfs,
dd onto a device, fork bombs, chmod -R 777 /, shutdown/reboot, killall
-9, userdel/groupdel on root/sudo/admin, iptables -F, writes onto common
block devices under /dev) always forces manual confirmation regardless
of auto-mode.
When a command is likely to ask for input, such as sudo, ssh, passwd,
an editor, or a pager, focus moves to the left pane so passwords and
other interactive prompts are entered into the shell running the
command. When the command finishes and the left pane returns to the
shell, focus moves back to the instruction pane. Non-interactive
commands keep focus on the instruction pane.
.SH FILES
No configuration files. Session context is kept in process memory for
the current tmux session and is not persisted by terminal-system.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR terminal-router (1)
